  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40(0)+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25{y}^{2}+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25\\,[{y}^{2}+4y+4] & =0 \\\\ 25\\,(y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/assets/78be4ca7215ee8dbf05a442080bae752e4fadfbe76084d975338d00e1bb02c78.png)",
